#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107127 :tutorial/content "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\cos   t \\\\ 2 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\sin   t \\\\ 2 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\cos   t \\\\ 3 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." :tutorial/id #uuid "f96a7b70-f713-5ac8-afbe-0bc9a8caf7b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5850 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107128 :tutorial/content "When curves are *negatively* oriented, the procedure of finding the outward-pointing unit normal vector is almost the same. \n\nThe only difference is the second step. If $C$ is negatively oriented (i.e., oriented *clockwise*), we must rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\n![](images/76/7641dfd05a69d6ffa2d0619d1de4a8d3bb6ec63b479b929d0f8e52bf910c9448.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "997b1386-6f87-5686-9745-a45b89423d14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5861 :tutorial/title "Case of Negatively Oriented Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107129 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n$$\n\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}^{\\prime } ( t ) \\\\ - {x}^{\\prime } ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d4a8dc89-6fbc-58b6-af1b-92bb93b085ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5864 :tutorial/title "Deriving the General Formula for a Positively Oriented Curve"}]}
